# The only process on the box that listens on a public port.
#
# Caddy terminates TLS, gets its own certificates from Let's Encrypt over HTTP-01, renews them
# unattended, and reverse-proxies to three containers that publish nothing to the host. Every
# hostname below must already resolve to this VM's public IP before the first start, because
# HTTP-01 works by Let's Encrypt fetching a token over port 80 — a DNS record added afterwards
# means the first issuance failed and Caddy is in a retry backoff.
#
# Hostnames come from the environment, not from this file, so one config serves the pilot domain
# and a staging one without an edit:
#
#     APP_DOMAIN=app.azmoth.app  API_DOMAIN=api.azmoth.app  MARKETING_DOMAIN=azmoth.app
#     ACME_EMAIL=ops@azmoth.app
#
# Caddy substitutes {$VAR} when it loads the config. An unset variable becomes an empty string and
# Caddy then refuses to start rather than serving an unnamed site, which is the failure you want.
#
# ── Why api.* is an allowlist rather than a reverse proxy ─────────────────────────────────────
# The engine authenticates nobody. `app/api/identity.py` and `app/api/tenancy.py` both say so at
# length: `X-User-ID` and `X-Organization-ID` are *asserted, not proven*, and what makes them
# trustworthy is the deployment shape — one proxy in front, the engine unreachable otherwise.
# tenancy.py puts it plainly: "A caller who can reach the engine directly can name any organisation
# they like ... which is exactly why the engine must not be exposed."
#
# So a blanket `reverse_proxy engine:8000` on a public hostname would hand the internet
# POST /api/v1/proposals and POST /api/v1/solve with a forgeable tenant header — anonymous writes
# into an append-only audit log, and any practice's drafts readable by naming their organisation.
#
# What IS safe to publish is the commercial surface: `/api/v1/audit/*` takes `RequestApiKey`, whose
# token is verified against the `api_keys` table on every call and whose tenant comes out of the
# stored row, so no header can name someone else's practice. That is the contract in
# docs/api/PARTNER_API.md, and it is exactly what this file exposes. Everything else on the engine
# answers 404 from here and is reachable only from inside the compose network — which is where
# `web` talks to it, over `ENGINE_BASE_URL=http://engine:8000`.
#
# Widening this is a security decision, not a configuration tweak. The change that earns it is the
# one identity.py names: a Better Auth JWT the engine verifies itself.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
	# Where Let's Encrypt sends expiry warnings if renewal has been failing. A real mailbox
	# somebody reads — this is the only warning before a certificate lapses.
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}

	# No admin API. It is bound to localhost by default, but this box runs a container with a
	# published port and there is nothing here that needs to reconfigure Caddy at runtime.
	admin off

	# One line per request, JSON, to stdout — so `docker compose logs caddy` is greppable the same
	# way the engine's log is. Caddy's default is also JSON; stated here so the format is a
	# property of this file rather than of a default that could change.
	log {
		output stdout
		format json
	}
}

# ── Snippets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Response headers every site gets. HSTS is deliberately NOT in here — see the note at app.* .
(hardening) {
	header {
		# Stop a browser from MIME-sniffing a JSON error body into something executable.
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		# This application is never framed. Clickjacking on an approval button is the concrete risk.
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		# Caddy advertises its version by default. It is not a vulnerability, it is a free hint.
		-Server
	}
}

# ── app.azmoth.app — the review and audit UI ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The only tier a browser talks to, and the tier that holds the Better Auth session. `web` reaches
# the engine itself over the compose network; nothing here proxies to `engine`.
{$APP_DOMAIN} {
	import hardening

	# HSTS, on the application host only. Not in the shared snippet, because `max-age` is a promise
	# a browser will not let you take back for its duration: if this domain ever has to serve plain
	# HTTP again, every browser that saw this header refuses to. It is right for the app — which is
	# HTTPS-only for the life of the deployment and sets `Secure` session cookies (see
	# `useSecureCookies` in apps/web/lib/auth.ts) — and it deliberately carries no `preload`, which
	# is the same promise made irrevocably and to every browser at once.
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"

	encode zstd gzip

	reverse_proxy web:3000 {
		# Caddy sets X-Forwarded-For, X-Forwarded-Proto and X-Forwarded-Host on its own, and the
		# web tier depends on all three: `trustedOrigins` in apps/web/lib/auth.ts compares the
		# browser's `Origin` against `x-forwarded-host`, and reads `x-forwarded-proto` to know
		# which scheme to trust. Without them every sign-in answers 403 INVALID_ORIGIN. They are
		# not restated here — Caddy would set them anyway, and a hand-written copy is a thing that
		# drifts.

		# The container's own healthcheck already gates compose startup; this gates *routing*, so a
		# web container that is up but not yet serving gets a 502 from Caddy rather than a hung
		# request. Same endpoint the compose healthcheck uses, deliberately: it asks Next.js
		# whether it can serve, and is not a proxy to the engine.
		health_uri /api/health
		health_interval 10s
		health_timeout 5s
	}
}

# ── api.azmoth.app — the partner API, and nothing else ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Read the long note at the top of this file before adding a path here.
{$API_DOMAIN} {
	import hardening

	encode zstd gzip

	# A hostile client should not be able to fill the data disk with a single POST. The engine's
	# own limits are 5 MiB for a single delivery and 50 MB for a bulk archive (apps/engine/app/api/
	# audit.py), and it answers those with a structured `REQUEST_TOO_LARGE` an integrator can act
	# on. This cap is set well above both so the engine's message is what a caller normally sees;
	# Caddy's blunter 413 is the backstop for something an order of magnitude out.
	request_body {
		max_size 64MB
	}

	# The allowlist. `handle` blocks are mutually exclusive and matched in order, so the final
	# bare `handle` catches everything not named above it.

	# The commercial surface. Every endpoint under it takes `RequestApiKey`: the token in
	# `X-API-Key` is verified against the `api_keys` table on each request and the tenant comes out
	# of the stored row, so this is safe to publish in a way the rest of the engine is not.
	handle /api/v1/audit* {
		reverse_proxy engine:8000
	}

	# Liveness, for an uptime monitor. Read-only, no tenant, no data — it reports whether Soufflé
	# loaded and the catalog is present.
	handle /api/v1/health {
		reverse_proxy engine:8000
	}

	# The machine-readable contract and its interactive copy, for an integrator wiring up a client.
	# Both are static descriptions of the API, not access to it.
	#
	# Serving /docs also serves the "Try it out" button, which will fail against every endpoint an
	# integrator has no key for — that is correct and is the point.
	handle /openapi.json {
		reverse_proxy engine:8000
	}
	handle /docs* {
		reverse_proxy engine:8000
	}

	# Everything else on the engine — /api/v1/solve, /api/v1/proposals/*, /api/v1/padnext/*,
	# /api/v1/settings/*, /api/v1/rules/*, /api/v1/demo/* — is the web application's own and
	# authenticates by an asserted header. It is not published.
	#
	# 404 rather than 403: a 403 confirms the path exists, which tells someone probing this host
	# exactly which endpoints to come back to when the auth model changes.
	handle {
		respond "Not Found" 404
	}
}

# ── azmoth.app and www.azmoth.app — the public marketing site ─────────────────────────────────
#
# Holds no data, talks to nothing, has no session. It is in this file because it is the other thing
# with a public hostname, not because it is part of the application.
#
# The apex redirects to www so there is one canonical origin — `MARKETING_SITE_URL` builds the
# sitemap and every og:url from a single value, and two origins serving identical pages is what
# splits them.
{$MARKETING_DOMAIN} {
	redir https://www.{$MARKETING_DOMAIN}{uri} permanent
}

www.{$MARKETING_DOMAIN} {
	import hardening
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy marketing:3000
}
